<Text>&lt;P style=&quot;BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 0.75pt solid; BORDER-LEFT: medium none; PADDING-BOTTOM: 1pt; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt; PADDING-LEFT: 0in; PADDING-RIGHT: 0in; BORDER-TOP: medium none; BORDER-RIGHT: medium none; PADDING-TOP: 0in&quot; class=MsoTitle align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT size=5 face=Arial&gt;RF Link Demo&amp;nbsp;Workspace&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoNormal align=center&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-FAMILY: Arial; FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;Keysight Technologies&amp;nbsp;Example Workspace&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;?xml:namespace prefix = o ns = &quot;urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office&quot; /&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoNormal align=center&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;BLOCKQUOTE style=&quot;MARGIN-RIGHT: 0px&quot; dir=ltr&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;ABSTRACT&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;This workspace illustrates&amp;nbsp;a tops-down DSP/RF design flow that combines Data Flow (signal processing simulation, included with the SystemVue environment) with Spectrasys (RF system tool, found in the W1719 RF Design Kit add-on product).&amp;nbsp; RF sections for&amp;nbsp;the Transmit (TX) and Receive (RX) functions are characterized and then pass&amp;nbsp;realistic communications Layer 1 signals.&amp;nbsp; This&amp;nbsp;provides greater RF accuracy to baseband simulations, and greater signal complexity to Spectrasys.&amp;nbsp; It also allows the Baseband and RF teams to use each other&apos;s engineering data/designs directly and concurrently.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;You might want to print this page for easy reference as you explore this example.&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/BLOCKQUOTE&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#0000ff size=5&gt;Combined RF/DSP Electronic System-Level (ESL) Design Flow&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT size=4&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Demo 1: DATAFLOW: Design DSP algorithm for comms PHY signal (QPSK)&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Design the&amp;nbsp;QPSK signal.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Define RF TX IF frequency.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Run analysis &quot;DF_QPSK_Source&quot;.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;The graph &apos;DS_QPSK_Source_SpecMod_Power&apos; show the generated&amp;nbsp;QPSK RF spectrum.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; dir=ltr class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Demo 2: SPECTRASYS: Design the RF Transmit system,&amp;nbsp;and evaluate its broadband frequency response.&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;UL style=&quot;MARGIN-RIGHT: 0px&quot; dir=ltr&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI dir=ltr&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;The RF TX system is a single mixer upconverter design that takes an IF input frequency at 220 MHz and outputs&amp;nbsp;an RF frequency at 1960 MHz.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI dir=ltr&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;First task is to create an RF TX design that does not contain undesirable levels of intermods, harmonics, or other spurious in the RF TX output information bandwidth.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI dir=ltr&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Characterize the bandpass response for this design; Gain vs Frequency In, Power In; Output noise vs Freq.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI dir=ltr&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Run analysis &quot;TX_Section_Analysis&quot;.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI dir=ltr&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;The graph &apos;TX_Section_PTX_RF_output&apos; shows the broadband frequency response with the fundamental and all generated intermodulation (IM) products&amp;nbsp;and harmonics.&amp;nbsp; Expand the spectrum about the fundamental output 1960 MHz frequency and observe the level of IM products produced.&amp;nbsp; The odd order IM products due to the nonlinearities are clearly visible (displayed with wider spectral widths since the fundamental was defined to have a spectral width of 1 MHz).&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI dir=ltr&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;The graph &apos;TX_Section_Data_EntirePath_CP&apos; shows the main signal path gain profile with an overall gain of 45 dB with display of the progressive gain along the signal path.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI dir=ltr&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;The graph &apos;TX_Section_Data_EntirePath_COMP&apos; shows the amount of gain compression for each component along the main signal path.&amp;nbsp; RFAmp_3 has the greates gain compression at 0.5 dB.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Demo 3: DATAFLOW: Evaluate the combined DSP/RF Transmit design for its bandpass time domain (complex envelope) response with the comms PHY signal&lt;/STRONG&gt;.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;UL style=&quot;MARGIN-RIGHT: 0px&quot; dir=ltr&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Use&amp;nbsp;RF_Link to embed the RF&amp;nbsp;TX system designed in Demo 2 within a Data Flow bandpass analysis and combined with the&amp;nbsp;QPSK signal designed in Demo 1.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Ensure&amp;nbsp;acceptable degradation of the comms PHY signal quality.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Run analysis &quot;QPSK_RF_TX_test&quot;.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Signal quality can be observed by measuring the RF TX output spectrum (looking at spectral regrowth due to nonlinearities) and EVM performance.&amp;nbsp; &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Notice the use of the AddNDensity at the output of the Modulator to set the noise density for the signal into the RF TX system at -174 dBm/Hz.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Optionally, the user can use a floating point to fixed point converter at the input of the Modulator with possible choices including AtoD, AtoD_ADI, FltToFxp models.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;The graph &apos;QPSK_RF_TX_SpecIn_Power&apos; shows the&amp;nbsp;QPSK RF spectrum at the input of the RF TX system.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;The graph &apos;QPSK_RF_TX_SpecOut_Power&apos; shows the&amp;nbsp;QPSK RF spectrum at the output of the RF TX system and shows the spectral sideband growth due to the nonlinearities in the RF TX system and includes the noise from the RF TX system.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;The table &apos;QPSK_RF_TX_EVM&apos; shows the&amp;nbsp;EVM measurement at the input (REF_EVM_RMS) and output (RFLINK_EVM_RMS) of the RF TX system (EVM values are in %).&amp;nbsp; As expected, the output EVM is degraded, but within an acceptable level to validate the design of the RF TX system as acceptable for use in an overall&amp;nbsp;QPSK comms system.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; dir=ltr class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Demo 4: SPECTRASYS:&amp;nbsp; Transmit path is done, now design the RF Receiver system and evaluate its broadband frequency response .&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI dir=ltr&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;The RF RX system is a single mixer downconverter design that takes a primary RF input frequency at 1960 MHz and outputs an IF frequency at 150 MHz.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Tones are used&amp;nbsp;to represent three RF RX input adjacent channel PHY signals; it also includes&amp;nbsp;an input tone for an input image signal.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Create an RF Receiver design that does not contain undesirable intermods, harmonics, or other spurious in the RF RX output information bandwidth.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI dir=ltr&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Characterize the bandpass response for this design;&amp;nbsp; Gain vs Frequency In, Power In; Output noise vs Freq&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI dir=ltr&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Run the analysis &quot;RX_Section_Analysis&quot;.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI dir=ltr&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;The graph &apos;RX_Section_PRxOut&apos; shows the broadband frequency response with the fundamental and all generated intermodulation (IM) products&amp;nbsp;and harmonics.&amp;nbsp; Expand the spectrum about the fundamental output 150 MHz frequency and observe the level of IM products produced.&amp;nbsp; The&amp;nbsp;IM products and harmonics due to the nonlinearities and mixer are clearly visible.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI dir=ltr&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;The graph &apos;RX_Section_Data_EntirePath_CP&apos; shows the main signal path gain profile with an overall gain of approximately 104 dB with display of the progressive gain along the signal path.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI dir=ltr&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;The graph &apos;RX_Section_Data_EntirePath_COMP&apos; shows the amount of gain compression for each component along the main signal path.&amp;nbsp; RFAmp_3 has the greatest gain compression at 0.16 dB.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Demo 5: DATAFLOW: Evaluate&amp;nbsp;the&amp;nbsp;combined DSP/RF Receiver design for its bandpass time domain (complex envelope) response with the comms PHY signals.&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;UL style=&quot;MARGIN-RIGHT: 0px&quot; dir=ltr&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Use&amp;nbsp;RF_Link to embed the RF&amp;nbsp;RX system designed in Demo 4 within a Data Flow bandpass analysis. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Include&amp;nbsp;the three input adjacent channel PHY signals and an image signal.&lt;BR&gt;Ensure&amp;nbsp;acceptable degradation of the comms PHY signal quality.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Run the analysis &quot;QPSK_RF_RX_test&quot;.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;Signal quality can be observed by measuring the RF RX output spectrum (looking at spectral regrowth due to nonlinearities) and BER performance.&amp;nbsp; &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;BER is not measured in this design.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;The graph &apos;QPSK_RF_RX_SpecIn_Power&apos; shows the&amp;nbsp;QPSK RF spectrum at the input of the RF RX system including adjacent channel&amp;nbsp;QPSK signals.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;The graph &apos;QPSK_RF_RX_SpecOut_Power&apos; shows the&amp;nbsp;QPSK RF spectrum at the output of the RF RX system (red trace) as well as the noise from just the RF RX system (blue trace). Observe&amp;nbsp;the spectral sideband growth due to the nonlinearities in the RF RX system and&amp;nbsp;the filtering of the adjacent channel&amp;nbsp;QPSK signals.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; class=MsoBodyText&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;</Text>
